@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Ski Gear";
}

<h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>
<p class="lead">The right equipment makes all the difference on the mountain.</p>

<div class="row mt-4">
    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Skis</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Skis come in several categories based on terrain and skill level:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>All-Mountain:</strong> Versatile skis that perform well on groomed runs and in varying conditions. Great for most recreational skiers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Carving Skis:</strong> Shorter with a pronounced sidecut, excellent for precise turns on groomed runs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Powder Skis:</strong> Wide underfoot with a rockered tip to float on deep powder. Best for off-piste and backcountry.</li>
                    <li><strong>Park &amp; Pipe:</strong> Twin-tip design for skiing forward and backward; built for terrain parks and halfpipes.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted"><small>Ski length is typically chosen based on height, weight, and skill level.</small></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Boots &amp; Bindings</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Ski boots are the most critical piece of equipment, a poor fit ruins any ski day.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Flex Rating:</strong> Soft flex (60-80) for beginners; stiff flex (100-130+) for advanced/aggressive skiers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Last Width:</strong> Boots come in narrow, medium, and wide to fit different foot shapes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bindings:</strong> DIN settings must be calibrated to skier weight, height, boot sole length, and skill level for safe release.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted"><small>Always have boots and bindings professionally fitted and adjusted.</small></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Helmet &amp; Goggles</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Safety gear is non-negotiable on the mountain.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Helmet:</strong> Look for ASTM F2040 or CE EN 1077 certification. MIPS (Multi-directional Impact Protection System) adds rotational impact protection.</li>
                    <li><strong>Goggles:</strong> Lens tint matters, rose/amber for low light, dark mirror lenses for bright sun. Photochromic lenses adapt automatically.</li>
                    <li><strong>OTG Goggles:</strong> Over-the-glasses goggles available for eyeglass wearers.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Clothing &amp; Layers</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Layering is the key to staying comfortable all day on the mountain.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Base Layer:</strong> Moisture-wicking merino wool or synthetic material. Avoid cotton.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mid Layer:</strong> Insulating fleece or down vest/jacket for warmth.</li>
                    <li><strong>Outer Shell:</strong> Waterproof and windproof jacket and pants. Look for taped seams and high waterproof ratings (10,000mm+).</li>
                    <li><strong>Accessories:</strong> Warm gloves or mittens, wool socks (ski-specific), balaclava or neck gaiter.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
